From: shirogane <artoriapendragon123@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] sunxi: D1s DRAM geometry, musb register map and clock
 fixes

musb_regs.h picks the sunxi register layout on CONFIG_ARCH_SUNXI, which is
an ARM-only symbol -- the RISC-V sunxi SoCs declare BOARD_SUNXI instead. The
D1 therefore got the generic map, where POWER is at 0x01 rather than 0x40
and FADDR at 0x00 rather than 0x98, so every access landed somewhere else.
Nothing complains: musb_start() writes POWER and INTRUSBE, they read back as
zero, the controller is never configured, and the gadget appears to the host
as a full-speed device that then answers no control transfer at all. With
the guard widened, POWER reads f0 (ISOUPDATE|SOFTCONN|HSENAB|HSMODE) and
DEVCTL 98, the device enumerates at high speed and dfu-util transfers
verify byte for byte.

The D1s carries in-package DDR2 at 1.8V where the T113-s3 has DDR3, so the
DRAM driver gains parameterised timings, a structured rank geometry and AC
remapping. Three bugs fixed along the way:

  - mctl_com_init() indexed config->ranks[] with the dram_para1 bit offset
    instead of the rank number, so a dual-rank part read past the array.
    Single-rank hardware never reaches the second iteration, which is why
    it went unnoticed.
  - The dram_para1 defaults in init_DRAM() (row 13 / page 2 / bank 1) and
    in auto_scan_dram_rank_width() (row 11 / page 0 / bank 0) had been
    collapsed into one value. Equivalent when autoscan runs and overwrites
    it, wrong when dram_tpr13 says the size is already known.
  - BA2 probing at A22 does not work on DDR2; it now picks A11 for DDR2
    and leaves the other types at A22 so T113/R528 DDR3 is unaffected.

The driver addresses registers as bare physical integers, which ARM's
readl/writel accept and RISC-V's do not. Rather than redefine the
accessors, wrap them in self-referential macros -- a function-like macro is
not expanded again inside its own expansion -- under CONFIG_RISCV only.

musb gains a has_sram flag instead of the unconditional call to
USBC_ConfigFIFO_Base(). All four existing ARM configs set it, so their
behaviour is unchanged; D1 does not. This matters because the syscon at
0x3000000 is not a USB FIFO mux on D1 -- it assigns SRAM C between the CPU,
VE and DE, and writing it pulls memory out from under whoever holds it.
D1's dts falls back to allwinner,sun8i-a33-musb, so the D1 compatible is
added ahead of it to win the match.

The clock changes add the SPL-stage CCU hook that raises PLL_CPUX to
1008MHz, and sunxi_mmc.c gets D1 onto the same PLL6 halving path as R528.

spi-sunxi.c has the same wrinkle as the DRAM driver, found the same way: its
SPI_REG() macro yields a bare u32 physical address, which ARM's accessors
take and RISC-V's do not. Cast in the macro rather than at every call site.
---
diff --git a/drivers/clk/sunxi/clk_d1.c b/drivers/clk/sunxi/clk_d1.c
index b990a118..fba5583b 100644
--- a/drivers/clk/sunxi/clk_d1.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/sunxi/clk_d1.c
@@ -6,6 +6,7 @@
 #include <clk-uclass.h>
 #include <dm.h>
 #include <errno.h>
+#include <asm/io.h>
 #include <clk/sunxi.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/clock/sun20i-d1-ccu.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/reset/sun20i-d1-ccu.h>
@@ -75,9 +76,21 @@ static struct ccu_reset d1_resets[] = {
 	[RST_BUS_LRADC]		= RESET(0xa9c, BIT(16)),
 };
 
+static void d1_ccu_init(struct udevice *dev)
+{
+	struct ccu_plat *plat = dev_get_plat(dev);
+
+	/*
+	 * Raise PLL_CPUX to 1008 MHz. Nothing claims the CPU clock through DM,
+	 * so without this the core stays at the boot ROM's default rate.
+	 */
+	clrsetbits_le32(plat->base + 0x000, 0xff << 8, 0x29 << 8);
+}
+
 const struct ccu_desc d1_ccu_desc = {
 	.gates	= d1_gates,
 	.resets	= d1_resets,
 	.num_gates = ARRAY_SIZE(d1_gates),
 	.num_resets = ARRAY_SIZE(d1_resets),
+	.init = d1_ccu_init,
 };
